<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: "Edit student",
                       description: "Changes apply everywhere this student appears." } do %><% end %>

  <%= form_with model: [@classroom, @student] do |f| %>
    <%= render "shared/form_errors", record: @student, subject: "student" %>

    <%# `.tw-card`, not a hand-rolled surface. This was `bg-white shadow-sm overflow-hidden lg:rounded-md`:
        no border at any width and no radius at all below lg, so on a phone the form sat on a white
        rectangle with square corners against a slate-50 page while every other panel in the app was a
        rounded-2xl bordered card.

        One column, not `grid grid-cols-6` with `col-span-6 lg:col-span-4`. Two fields do not need a
        twelve-column grid, and at lg that grid made the inputs two thirds of a container that is already
        capped at 672px. A single-column stack is what GOV.UK, Polaris and Tailwind UI use for a short
        form, and one field per row is what makes a form scannable. %>
    <div class="tw-card p-5 lg:p-6">
      <div class="space-y-5">
        <%# `users.name` has existed the whole time and `User#display_name` prefers it, but nothing ever
            collected it - so every student's name was nil and every screen fell back to a lowercased
            username. Reported as "why are all the student names lowercase": they were not names. Required
            here, on the form, rather than on every create: bulk CSV import takes a username and a
            classroom id and nothing else, so a model-wide requirement would fail every row of a class
            being onboarded. See Student. %>
        <div>
          <%= f.label :name, class: "tw-label-primary" do %>
            Full name<span class="required-indicator">*</span>
          <% end %>
          <%= f.text_field :name,
                           class: "tw-input-primary",
                           required: true,
                           placeholder: "e.g., Jordan Smith" %>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Shown instead of the username wherever this student appears. A roster of usernames alone is
            hard to read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <%= f.label :username, class: "tw-label-primary" do %>
            Username<span class="required-indicator">*</span>
          <% end %>
          <%= f.text_field :username, class: "tw-input-primary", required: true %>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
            What the student signs in with. Changing it changes how they sign in.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%# Actions on the **leading edge**, below the card, on the page background.

        They were in a `bg-slate-50 text-right` strip inside the card - reported as a grey background
        behind the buttons - and design.md is explicit: form actions anchor to the leading edge, never
        centred, which is where the classroom form and the grade book both put theirs. A tinted footer
        inside the card is Polaris's and Stripe's variant of the same rule, and it is a different
        decision, not a free one: it needs the card to own its own padding and it fills a strip with a
        colour that means nothing here. One shape for the product, and this is it.

        Primary first, because at the leading edge the eye reads left to right and the primary is what the
        form is for. Trailing alignment reverses that - Polaris and Stripe put Cancel first there - which
        is exactly why the two cannot be mixed. %>
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <%= f.submit "Update student", class: "tw-btn-primary" %>
      <%= link_to "Cancel", classroom_path(@classroom), class: "tw-btn-secondary" %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
